# KAMCH ERP — FINAL FEES MANAGEMENT MODULE IMPLEMENTATION PROMPT

## 1. Objective

Implement the Fees Management module for Krishna Ayurvedic Medical College and Hospital (KAMCH ERP).

This module must be practical, secure, maintainable, and suitable for actual college office use.

The existing Fees and User Management implementations were initially created for testing. Treat the Fees module requirements below as the authoritative specification. Inspect existing code only as needed to integrate the module safely. Modify or replace the existing Fees implementation where necessary.

**Do not rebuild the entire ERP. Do not conduct a separate audit. Do not add unrelated features.**

Implement only the requirements specified here.

---

## 2. Mandatory Working Rules

1. Work within the existing project architecture and technology stack.
2. Reuse the existing authentication, database connection, session management, authorization middleware, logging, and UI components wherever appropriate.
3. Do not break the login system, User Management, database connection, or other working foundation components.
4. Do not create duplicate student, user, or authentication systems.
5. Do not hardcode fee amounts, batch-specific fee values, concession values, or business rules in frontend or backend code.
6. All configurable fees must be stored in the database and managed through authorized interfaces.
7. Enforce permissions on the server/API, not just by hiding frontend buttons.
8. Use database transactions for operations that modify financial records.
9. Preserve historical financial records and assessments.
10. Do not delete or overwrite existing database tables. Use safe, additive migrations and adapt the existing schema where necessary.
11. Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies, dashboards, automation, or architectural complexity.
12. Implement the complete module before adding any other module.
13. Do not stop after creating UI mockups. Connect all implemented features to real database operations.
14. After implementation, run the available type checks, production build, and relevant tests. Fix errors introduced by the implementation.

